WITH items(slug,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,image_url,image_alt_text,body) AS (VALUES
('2026-08-09-abbott-cps-energy-competition','Energy','Abbott Pushes to Open San Antonio Power Market, Putting CPS Energy Model Under Pressure','Gov. Greg Abbott is calling for more electricity competition in San Antonio, setting up a 2027 fight over CPS Energy, city finances and consumer rates.','San Antonio Express-News','https://www.expressnews.com/news/article/abbott-cps-energy-break-up-economic-development-22377334.php','2026-08-09T09:26:00Z'::timestamptz,40,TRUE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/abbott-cps-energy-competition.svg','Editorial illustration of San Antonio power lines, a city skyline and competing electricity meters.','San Antonio is heading toward a major electricity-policy fight after Gov. Greg Abbott called for breaking open the city''s municipally owned power market to competition. CPS Energy has long operated as a city-owned monopoly, giving San Antonio direct control over generation, transmission, customer service and a utility payment that helps fund the city budget. Abbott argues that introducing retail competition could lower bills and give customers more choice. Local business leaders and city officials are pushing back, warning that the change could weaken one of San Antonio''s most important economic-development tools. CPS Energy transfers a share of its annual revenue to the city general fund, helping pay for police, fire, streets and other services. If customers were allowed to choose outside electricity providers, the value and structure of that transfer could change. Supporters of deregulation say Texans in competitive markets can shop for lower prices and products that better match their needs. Critics point to complicated rate plans, price volatility and the loss of local control as reasons San Antonio should keep its current model. The debate also reaches beyond household bills. CPS Energy has helped negotiate power arrangements for major employers and industrial projects, and business groups fear uncertainty around the utility could make recruiting companies more difficult. Abbott has suggested competition could reduce bills by as much as 13 percent, though San Antonio leaders are asking for more evidence behind that estimate. Any major restructuring would likely require legislative action in 2027, so nothing changes immediately for CPS customers. The next phase will center on rate comparisons, the city''s dependence on utility revenue, CPS debt and whether lawmakers believe San Antonio customers would be better served by the statewide retail model. For Texans outside San Antonio, the fight matters because it could reopen a broader question the state has largely left alone for years: whether large municipal utilities should remain exempt from retail electric competition.'),
('2026-08-09-usda-mexican-cattle-reopening','Agriculture','USDA Sets August 24 Start for Limited Mexican Cattle Imports as Screwworm Fight Continues','The first southern livestock port is scheduled to reopen in Arizona, a cautious step closely watched by Texas cattle producers after screwworm detections disrupted trade.','USDA APHIS','https://direct.aphis.usda.gov/news/agency-announcements/usda-announces-phased-reopening-southern-ports-livestock-trade-0','2026-08-09T09:27:00Z'::timestamptz,39,TRUE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/usda-mexican-cattle-reopening.svg','Editorial illustration of cattle crossing an inspected border livestock gate with a Texas ranch in the distance.','The U.S. Department of Agriculture is preparing a cautious reopening of livestock trade from Mexico, a move with direct consequences for Texas ranchers, feedlots and beef prices. USDA says the Douglas, Arizona, port of entry is scheduled to reopen to cattle on August 24 if Mexico continues meeting milestones in a joint New World screwworm control plan. Every animal entering through the reopened port will receive a USDA inspection for signs of the parasite. The reopening does not immediately restore cattle traffic through Texas. Southern livestock ports remain closed for now, and USDA says additional openings in New Mexico will depend on the results of the first phase and updated disease-risk assessments. Texas producers are watching closely because the state has already dealt with confirmed screwworm cases this year and because imported Mexican cattle are an important part of the regional beef supply chain. New World screwworm larvae feed on living tissue and can severely injure livestock, wildlife, pets and, in rare cases, people. The threat has forced federal and state agencies to balance two expensive risks: allowing trade to resume too quickly and potentially spreading the pest, or keeping ports closed long enough to strain cattle supplies and increase costs. USDA says Sonora and Chihuahua are currently considered lower-risk Mexican states because of their inspection programs and distance from the center of most active cases. The August 24 reopening is therefore being treated as a test rather than a return to normal trade. Texas cattle operators should not assume cross-border flows will immediately return to pre-closure levels. The pace will depend on inspections, new detections and whether Mexico meets control commitments. The broader significance is that the federal government now sees enough progress to attempt a limited reopening. If that succeeds, it could ease pressure on cattle supplies. If new cases appear closer to the border, the process could slow or reverse quickly.'),
('2026-08-09-texas-panhandle-5-earthquake','Texas','Magnitude 5.0 Earthquake Rattles the Texas Panhandle, Renewing Questions About a Changing Seismic Map','The strongest Panhandle quake in roughly five years was felt across a wide area and is drawing new attention to earthquake activity in Texas.','KSAT / Texas Tribune','https://www.ksat.com/news/texas/','2026-08-09T09:28:00Z'::timestamptz,38,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/texas-panhandle-5-earthquake.svg','Editorial illustration of the Texas Panhandle with seismic waves spreading beneath ranchland.','A magnitude 5.0 earthquake shook the Texas Panhandle early Thursday, strong enough to be felt across a wide area and unusual enough to renew attention on the state''s changing seismic landscape. Reports carried by Texas news outlets described it as the strongest earthquake in the Panhandle in about five years. A magnitude 5 event is capable of producing noticeable shaking over a broad region and can cause minor damage depending on depth, distance and building construction. Texas is not typically associated with large natural earthquakes, but parts of the state have seen a sharp rise in seismic activity over the past decade. West Texas has experienced the most sustained increase, where researchers and regulators have examined links between earthquakes and underground disposal of oil-field wastewater. The Panhandle has a different geology and industrial pattern, so a single event does not establish a cause. That distinction matters. Earthquake location, depth, aftershock patterns and nearby injection activity all have to be evaluated before scientists can responsibly connect a quake to human activity or a natural fault. Residents who felt the shaking should document any damage and watch for official aftershock guidance. Even moderate earthquakes can reveal weaknesses in masonry, foundations, utility connections and older structures. For Texas regulators, the event is another reminder that earthquake preparedness is no longer only a distant concern. The Railroad Commission has already created seismic response areas in parts of West Texas and can limit disposal activity when evidence links injection practices to increased earthquake risk. Whether the Panhandle quake leads to additional review will depend on the scientific data that follows. The immediate takeaway is simpler: a 5.0 quake is significant by Texas standards, and residents, emergency managers and energy operators will be watching closely for aftershocks and for a clearer explanation of what happened beneath the Panhandle.'),
('2026-08-09-harris-county-ice-shooting-investigation','Houston','Harris County Approves $2.5 Million Local Investigation Into Fatal ICE Shooting','Commissioners voted unanimously to fund a three-year investigation into the killing of Lorenzo Salgado Araujo as local prosecutors seek evidence from federal authorities.','Houston Chronicle','https://www.houstonchronicle.com/politics/houston/article/harris-county-lorenzo-salgado-araujo-22371668.php','2026-08-09T09:29:00Z'::timestamptz,38,TRUE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/harris-county-ice-shooting-investigation.svg','Editorial illustration of a Harris County courthouse, evidence files and federal-local jurisdiction lines.','Harris County commissioners have approved up to $2.5 million for a local investigation into the fatal shooting of Lorenzo Salgado Araujo by a federal immigration officer, an unusually large commitment by a county government to scrutinize a federal use-of-force case. The money is expected to support the district attorney''s work over three years, including legal costs, staff time, technology and other expenses tied to a complicated investigation. District Attorney Sean Teare has said federal authorities have not provided all of the evidence his office wants, creating a jurisdictional and practical challenge for local investigators. The case has drawn intense attention because witnesses dispute parts of the federal account of the July shooting and because the officers involved did not have body-camera footage available from the encounter. One key witness was later ordered released from immigration detention by a federal judge. Commissioners from both parties backed the funding, framing the vote as a transparency and accountability measure rather than a predetermined judgment about criminal liability. That distinction is important. Funding an investigation does not mean prosecutors have concluded that the shooting was unlawful, and federal agents can have legal defenses that differ from those available in ordinary local cases. The money gives Harris County the ability to gather evidence, retain outside expertise if necessary and continue working even if the federal process moves slowly. It also signals that local officials do not intend to treat the matter solely as a federal internal review. For Houston residents, the case is likely to become a test of how much authority local prosecutors can exercise when a federal agent uses deadly force inside the county. The investigation could take months or years and may end without charges. But the unanimous vote ensures that cost and staffing will be less likely to determine how aggressively the county pursues the facts.'),
('2026-08-09-houston-sunday-storms-heat','Weather','Houston Faces Sunday Storm Window Before Another Stretch of Dangerous Midweek Heat','Scattered Gulf storms could reach the metro Sunday afternoon, but forecasters expect hotter and drier conditions to rebuild by midweek.','Houston Chronicle','https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-weather/forecast/article/houston-hot-storms-gulf-rain-weather-22380191.php','2026-08-09T09:30:00Z'::timestamptz,34,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/houston-sunday-storms-heat.svg','Editorial illustration of Houston skyline under afternoon thunderstorms with a heat gauge rising behind it.','Houston has a narrow chance for useful rain Sunday before high pressure rebuilds and pushes Southeast Texas back toward more dangerous heat. Forecasts call for scattered Gulf thunderstorms to move inland during the afternoon, with the best window roughly between 1 and 5 p.m. Rain chances are generally around 30 to 40 percent, meaning many neighborhoods may stay dry while a few locations receive brief heavy downpours. The bigger concern is what follows. Highs Sunday are expected in the mid-90s, and humidity could make it feel near 102 degrees. By midweek, temperatures are forecast to climb into the upper 90s with heat index values potentially reaching 105 to 108. Warm overnight lows near 80 will limit the time people and buildings have to cool down between hot afternoons. Houston has recently experienced a dry stretch despite running above normal for year-to-date rainfall, so isolated storms may provide short-term relief without changing the broader pattern. Any thunderstorm can also bring lightning and sudden street flooding, especially where heavy rain falls quickly. Families preparing for the start of school should pay particular attention to afternoon conditions because the combination of heat, school pickup traffic, outdoor athletics and scattered storms can create rapidly changing risks. Texas schools and athletic programs are also operating under updated heat-safety practices that rely more heavily on wet-bulb globe temperature measurements. The forecast does not point to a major regional severe-weather event. Instead, it is a familiar Gulf Coast combination: spotty storms, high humidity and a quick return to oppressive heat. Residents should use the rain window as a temporary break rather than assume a broader cool-down is coming. Hydration, shade and limiting strenuous outdoor activity during the hottest part of the day will become increasingly important as the week progresses.'),
('2026-08-09-rangers-fourth-straight-degrom','Sports','Rangers Win Fourth Straight Behind deGrom as AL West Race Tightens','Jacob deGrom struck out nine and Corey Seager supplied a late three-run homer in a 5-1 win over Baltimore, moving Texas closer to Houston in the division race.','Reuters','https://www.reuters.com/sports/baseball/jacob-degrom-continues-dominance-os-rangers-win-4th-straight-game--flm-2026-08-09/','2026-08-09T09:31:00Z'::timestamptz,31,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/rangers-fourth-straight-degrom.svg','Editorial illustration of a Texas baseball pitcher, four-game winning streak marks and a tightening division standings board.','The Texas Rangers have turned a late-summer series against Baltimore into their strongest stretch in weeks, winning a fourth straight game as the American League West race suddenly gets much tighter. Jacob deGrom allowed one run on three hits over five innings and struck out nine in Saturday night''s 5-1 victory. The performance continued his strong history against Baltimore and gave Texas another quality start at a moment when every game is beginning to carry postseason weight. The Rangers scored twice in the second inning after Orioles defensive mistakes created extra opportunities. The game stayed close until Corey Seager broke it open with a three-run homer in the eighth. Texas relievers then finished four scoreless innings to protect the lead. The win matters beyond the streak itself. Houston''s loss in San Diego reduced the Astros'' division lead over Texas to only half a game, putting the two Texas clubs nearly even as the schedule moves deeper into August. That changes the emotional temperature around every series. A week ago, the Rangers were trying to stabilize a season that had repeatedly drifted around the .500 mark. Now a handful of wins have put first place within immediate reach. DeGrom''s health remains one of the biggest variables for Texas. When he is available and missing bats, the Rangers can match almost any opponent at the front of the rotation. The question is whether the club can keep getting enough offense and bullpen support to turn strong starts into wins. Four straight victories do not guarantee anything in a crowded playoff race, but they have changed the standings quickly. For Rangers fans, the next few games are no longer just about staying relevant. Texas has a realistic chance to overtake Houston and turn the final weeks into a head-to-head fight for the division.'),
('2026-08-09-bucees-john-oliver-beaver-lawsuit','Viral','Buc-ee’s Trademark Fight Goes National After John Oliver Dares the Texas Chain to Sue Him','The Texas travel-center giant is suing an Ohio mini mart over a beaver logo while John Oliver’s parody campaign turns the dispute into a national viral story.','The Verge','https://www.theverge.com/business/977112/buc-ees-john-oliver-lawsuit-beaver-mini-mart','2026-08-09T09:32:00Z'::timestamptz,29,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/bucees-john-oliver-beaver-lawsuit.svg','Editorial illustration of two cartoon beaver-style signs facing off across a courthouse with parody television spotlights.','Buc-ee''s aggressive defense of its famous beaver branding has become a national comedy story after John Oliver publicly challenged the Texas travel-center chain and a new trademark lawsuit pulled a small Ohio store into the spotlight. Buc-ee''s sued Beaver''s Mini Mart in Beavercreek, Ohio, alleging that the local store''s smiling beaver logo and red branding are similar enough to create consumer confusion. The Ohio business predates Buc-ee''s arrival in the area, but Buc-ee''s argues its registered trademarks give it broad protection against confusingly similar convenience-store branding. The lawsuit might have remained a niche trademark dispute if not for Last Week Tonight. Oliver recently mocked Buc-ee''s history of enforcing its trademarks and created parody merchandise built around a squirrel character and a deliberately provocative Buc-ee''s spoof. He publicly invited the chain to sue him. Buc-ee''s has not taken that bait, but its case against Beaver''s Mini Mart has now received national coverage and inspired local fundraising support for the smaller business. Trademark law is more complicated than comparing two cartoon animals side by side. Courts typically examine the strength of the mark, similarity, the products and services involved, actual confusion and other factors. Companies also have a legitimate reason to police trademarks because failing to defend a brand can weaken protection over time. Still, Buc-ee''s public image makes the case unusually combustible. The chain has built a devoted following around oversized stores, clean bathrooms, barbecue, snacks and its instantly recognizable mascot. That same mascot now places the company in the awkward position of explaining why another smiling beaver is a legal threat. For Texans, the viral fight is a reminder that Buc-ee''s is no longer merely a quirky home-state institution. It is a national brand with national legal exposure, and every lawsuit can now become part of the brand story.'),
('2026-08-09-wembanyama-nba2k27-cover','Viral','Victor Wembanyama Lands NBA 2K27 Standard Cover as Spurs Star’s Global Profile Keeps Growing','San Antonio’s Victor Wembanyama will front the standard edition of NBA 2K27, putting the Spurs centerpiece on one of basketball culture’s most visible covers.','2K','https://newsroom-nl.2k.com/news/nbar-2k27-cover-athletes-revealed-victor-wembanyama-caitlin-clark-and-derrick-rose','2026-08-09T09:33:00Z'::timestamptz,28,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/wembanyama-nba2k27-cover.svg','Editorial illustration of a towering San Antonio basketball player framed inside a video-game cover silhouette and city lights.','Victor Wembanyama''s rise from prized prospect to the face of the San Antonio Spurs is now showing up on one of basketball''s most recognizable pieces of pop culture. 2K selected Wembanyama as the standard-edition cover athlete for NBA 2K27, placing him alongside Caitlin Clark on the Deluxe Edition and Derrick Rose on the limited Ultra Edition lineup. The game launches September 4, with early access beginning August 28 for certain editions. The cover is more than a marketing photo. NBA 2K has become a major part of how younger fans encounter players, teams, shoes and basketball culture, and the standard edition is typically the version seen most widely on digital storefronts and retail shelves. For San Antonio, the choice reinforces how quickly Wembanyama has become an international representative of the franchise. 2K highlighted his impact as a defender and his unusual combination of size and skill. That tracks with the broader narrative around the Spurs star: he is not only expected to lead San Antonio competitively, but to help pull the team back into the center of the NBA''s national conversation. The timing is especially useful for the Spurs as the organization enters a period of heightened attention around roster building, arena planning and the next stage of Wembanyama''s career. Video-game covers can become snapshots of an era, and San Antonio fans will likely treat this one as a collectible marker of his ascent. There is also a practical fan angle. The gameplay trailer has already featured Wembanyama, Clark and Rose, while the full game release arrives just before NBA training camps begin. That keeps the Spurs star visible during the quietest stretch of the basketball calendar. For a player whose reach already extends well beyond Texas, the 2K27 cover adds another mainstream platform to a rapidly expanding profile.'),
('2026-08-09-san-antonio-firefighter-childrens-book','Viral','San Antonio Firefighter Turns Nine Years on the Job Into a Children’s Book for Future Girls in the Firehouse','Firefighter Hannah Detmers wrote and illustrated a story designed to show young girls that firefighting can be part of their future.','KSAT','https://www.ksat.com/features/','2026-08-09T09:34:00Z'::timestamptz,25,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/san-antonio-firefighter-childrens-book.svg','Editorial illustration of a San Antonio firefighter reading a bright children’s book beside a fire engine and young readers.','A San Antonio firefighter is turning nearly a decade of real-world experience into a children''s story aimed at giving girls a picture of themselves in the fire service. Hannah Detmers, who has worked as a San Antonio firefighter for nine years, wrote and illustrated the fictional book Flora''s Dream, Flicker to Flame. The project is built around a simple idea: children are more likely to imagine themselves in a career when they can see someone like them doing it. Fire departments across Texas have spent years trying to broaden recruiting and build stronger pipelines into public-safety careers. Women remain a minority in firefighting, and the physical image of the profession is still often presented to children as overwhelmingly male. A book centered on a girl considering the job tackles that perception before it becomes fixed. Detmers'' approach is also unusually personal because she handled both the writing and illustration. Rather than presenting firefighting only as dramatic emergency response, a children''s book can introduce the teamwork, training, service and persistence behind the profession in a format families can discuss together. The story is gaining attention in San Antonio because it connects a local first responder with a broader representation issue. It also arrives during back-to-school season, when teachers, libraries and parents are looking for books tied to careers and community helpers. No single book will change fire-department demographics, but small cultural signals matter. A child who sees a woman driving a fire engine, wearing turnout gear or leading a crew may be less likely to treat that career as off limits. Detmers has already spent nine years proving the point in person. Her book gives that example a way to reach children who may never meet her at a station or school event.'),
('2026-08-09-san-antonio-water-splash-road-rage','Viral','A Splash of Water Allegedly Triggered a San Antonio Freeway Shootout With a 2-Year-Old in the Back Seat','Court records describe an extraordinary road-rage case in which a minor traffic splash escalated into gunfire while a toddler was riding in one of the vehicles.','KSAT','https://www.ksat.com/news/local/2026/07/27/sapd-splashed-water-on-vehicle-spurs-apparent-road-rage-shooting-with-2-year-old-caught-in-middle/','2026-08-09T09:35:00Z'::timestamptz,27,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/san-antonio-water-splash-road-rage.svg','Editorial illustration of two vehicles separated by a splash of water, freeway lanes and a child-safety seat warning symbol.','A San Antonio road-rage case is drawing renewed attention because police say an almost trivial roadway interaction escalated into gunfire with a 2-year-old child sitting in the back seat of one of the vehicles. According to court records reported by KSAT, the confrontation began after water splashed onto another vehicle. Investigators say the dispute continued onto Interstate 10 and eventually became an exchange of gunfire between vehicles. Daniel Jude Jimenez Jr. and Lois Badillo-Lopez were arrested and face charges that include aggravated assault for allegedly firing from a vehicle and child endangerment. The presence of the toddler makes the case particularly disturbing. Police say the child was inside the suspects'' vehicle during the confrontation, turning an already dangerous road-rage episode into a situation where a young passenger could have been struck by gunfire or hurt in a crash. The allegations remain to be proven in court, and charges are not convictions. Still, the circumstances have spread widely because they illustrate how quickly ordinary driving frustration can become life-threatening. Texas transportation and law-enforcement agencies repeatedly warn drivers not to pursue, confront or retaliate against aggressive motorists. The safest response is usually to create distance, avoid eye contact or gestures, move to a public place if followed and call 911 when there is an immediate threat. The case also highlights a practical point for parents: a child in the car does not reduce the risk created by an escalating confrontation; it multiplies it. A freeway argument that begins with a splash, lane change or horn can put everyone nearby in danger once drivers decide to continue the conflict. San Antonio police are treating this as a serious felony case, but its viral power comes from the absurdity of the alleged trigger compared with the consequences. A little water on a vehicle should have ended as an annoyance. Investigators say it instead became a shooting.' )
